    "['mpls ldp', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of enabling LDP on interfaces is typically done by configuring `ldp` under `protocols` and specifying the interfaces (or `all` for all interfaces). The Huawei `mpls ldp` command enables LDP globally and on interfaces where it's configured, while Juniper uses a more hierarchical approach with explicit interface assignments. The translation assumes you want LDP enabled on all interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where it's enabled on GE1/0/0 and GE1/0/1). If specific interfaces are needed, they would be listed instead of `all`.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface all'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls ldp', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `mpls ldp` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\nset protocols ldp interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, MPLS LDP is enabled globally under `protocols ldp`\\n2. \"interface all\" enables LDP on all interfaces (similar to Huawei\\'s interface-level `mpls ldp` commands)\\n3. The loopback interface (lo0.0) is explicitly included as this is typically needed for LDP sessions\\n\\nNote: In a real deployment, you might want to be more specific about which interfaces should run LDP rather than using \"all\". The exact equivalent would depend on your network design requirements.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `mpls ldp` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\nset protocols ldp interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, MPLS LDP is enabled globally under `protocols ldp`\\n2. \"interface all\" enables LDP on all interfaces (similar to Huawei\\'s interface-level `mpls ldp` commands)\\n3. The loopback interface (lo0.0) is explicitly included as this is typically needed for LDP sessions\\n\\nNote: In a real deployment, you might want to be more specific about which interfaces should run LDP rather than using \"all\". The exact equivalent would depend on your network design requirements.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
